CORE
continue_order1.sv
--bound 2
^\[main\.p0\] always \(main\.c1 && main\.c2 \|-> \(##1 main\.x == 0\)\): PROVED up to bound 2$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
A loop body with two conditional `continue` statements: `x = 0; if (c1)
continue; x = 1; if (c2) continue; x = 2;`. Per IEEE 1800-2017 section
12.8 `continue` jumps immediately to the end of the current iteration, so
when c1 holds the first `continue` is taken, leaving x == 0, and the later
`continue` on c2 is never reached. Hence c1 && c2 must give x == 0.

Observed: p0 is REFUTED. With --show-rtl the register next-state value is
c2 ? 1 : (c1 ? 0 : 2) instead of the correct c1 ? 0 : (c2 ? 1 : 2), so
c1 && c2 yields x == 1.

Root cause: verilog_rtl_buildert::build_for in src/verilog/verilog_rtl.cpp
merges the recorded `continue_states` in forward program order, so a later
`continue` overrides an earlier one whose guard also holds. The analogous
`break_states` loop in the same function correctly merges in reverse
program order ("These come in program order, hence process in reverse
order").
